   +----------------+----------------+-----------------+--------------+

          Table 1: Derivation for the three deployment contexts

   In the Result column, &quot;Protection goal&quot; marks an obligation on the
   binding (Section 9), &quot;Left to the WG&quot; marks a question in Section 13,
   and &quot;Beyond the minimum&quot; marks information a client does not need for
   its next step, which is therefore not part of MDI (Section 8.3).  The
   Other source column reflects the representative mechanisms above.

   The results are the same in all three contexts; what differs is where
   the client gets each item.  On a local network, each result carries
   the name, the endpoint location, and the protocol.  A query for a
   known name, as under a cooperating party&#x27;s domain, supplies the name,
   and a query for one protocol supplies the protocol.  The binding
   supplies defaults, such as the protocol&#x27;s default path, and usually
   the endpoint purpose, which follows from the protocol or from a well-
   known name such as an index label.  Freshness comes from the lifetime
   of the records a result is built from.

   The conditional items depend on the next step.  A display name
   matters when a person chooses among results.  An authentication hint
   matters when the client interacts directly and neither a description
   nor the agent protocol&#x27;s own handshake supplies the scheme first.  A
   Stable Identifier matters when results are correlated, as when a
   client within an organization sees the same agent both through
   multicast DNS on its link and through a Discovery Proxy
   (Section 8.5).  Entity type matters when the client has to tell kinds
   of AI resource apart to choose its next step, and is usually implied
   by the carrier, such as a well-known name, or by the endpoint
   purpose.  Whether services are covered by Endpoint protocols and
   Entity Type is asked in Section 13.  Information beyond the minimum
   can help but is not needed for this initial step: the client chooses
   by protocol and type, learns the details from the description, and
   learns availability at the follow-up connection.

   Where the results are meant for an audience, as &quot;available to
   employees&quot; and &quot;for all its customers/members&quot; suggest, the
   restriction applies to the lookup, for example through split-view DNS
   or a VPN, and the result carries nothing that enforces it; where this
   requester context belongs is asked in Section 13.

<span>Cui &amp; Yao                  Expires 6 May 2027                  [Page 13]</span>
<span>Internet-Draft    Minimum Public Discovery Information     November 2026</span>

   Origin is where the contexts differ most.  Under a party&#x27;s domain,
   DNSSEC-validated records and TLS-authenticated origins tie the
   information to the party.  A catalog or a Discovery Proxy that relays
   information can be authenticated only as itself, and on a local link
   multicast DNS does not authenticate advertisements, so the agent is
   authenticated only at the follow-up connection
   [I-D.jakab-dawn-agent-discovery-mdns].  How the charter&#x27;s integrity
   requirement is met for such advertisements is asked in Section 13.

   Compared with REQ-PROP-1 of the individual requirements draft
   [I-D.king-dawn-requirements], reachability and protocol are needed in
   every context, type and security parameters (an authentication hint)
   under a condition, and services are a question for the working group.

8.  MDI Information Model

   An MDI instance is the common header published or obtained for one
   entity.  Its elements follow the results of Section 7 (Table 1):

   *  Information needed in every context becomes the mandatory Name and
      Endpoint (location, agent protocol, and purpose) and the Freshness
      element.  Freshness is SHOULD because its meaning is obtainable
      from the carrier lifetime; a binding whose carrier provides no
      lifetime MUST carry it.

   *  Information needed only under a condition becomes an element with
      Status MAY: Entity Type, Display Name, Authentication Hint, and
      Stable Identifier.  A publisher cannot always know whether the
      condition holds for a given consumer; a binding or profile for
      deployments where it holds can make the element MUST; for example,
      a binding for exchange between gateways or catalogs can make the
      Stable Identifier MUST.

   *  Services, left to the working group, are covered by Endpoint
      protocols and Entity Type (Section 8.4).

   *  Origin is a protection goal, met by the binding (Section 9), and
      is not an element.

   *  Information beyond the minimum is not an element of MDI.  A
      binding can still carry it as an Extension, and Section 8.3 names
      the kinds that existing formats and individual drafts carry, so
      that bindings agree on their meaning.

   Mandatory elements keep the record very small; in constrained
   carriers, a binding can derive their values from the carrier or from
   known context (Section 9).  All Status values are provisional.

<span>Cui &amp; Yao                  Expires 6 May 2027                  [Page 14]</span>
<span>Internet-Draft    Minimum Public Discovery Information     November 2026</span>

   Status key words in this document place obligations on the publishers
   and bindings that claim to carry MDI, and key words addressed to
   consumers apply to implementations of a binding.  Status says how
   strongly the meaning of an element has to be obtainable by the
   consumer, from the response, from known context, or from binding
   rules; whether each response carries a value explicitly is a binding
   decision.  Status applies to the instance a consumer obtains; a
   publisher provides every value that the binding neither derives nor
   fixes and that no serving system generates (Section 5).  Cardinality
   governs how many values of an element may be carried.  Obligations
   specific to binding documents are collected in Section 9.

8.1.  Structure

   Figure 2 shows the elements and their shapes.  Composite members
   marked &quot;?&quot; are optional, and freshness, whose members are all
   optional, is present only when at least one member is present; all
   other structure is given by the cardinalities.

   +--------------+   describes exactly one
   | MDI Instance |---------------------------&gt;  Entity
   +--------------+
     |- name                                        (1)
     |- endpoint { locator-type, locator,
     |             protocols (1..n), purpose?,
     |             digest? }                        (1..n)
     |- freshness { version?, valid-from?,
     |              valid-until?, refresh-hint? }   (0..1)
     |- entity-type                                 (0..1)
     |- display-name                                (0..n)
     |- authentication-hint { scheme, metadata? }   (0..n)
     |- stable-identifier                           (0..1)
     |- extension { name, value }                   (0..n)

     Reference = { ref-type, locator, digest? }

                    Figure 2: Elements of the MDI model

   A Reference is a typed pointer, used by the Authentication Hint and
   by information beyond the minimum: the ref-type says how to interpret
   the locator, and the optional digest is an integrity digest over the
   object the locator identifies (Section 8.5).  Information carried
   directly, rather than through a Reference, is covered by the
   integrity protection of the binding (Section 9).

<span>Cui &amp; Yao                  Expires 6 May 2027                  [Page 15]</span>
<span>Internet-Draft    Minimum Public Discovery Information     November 2026</span>

8.2.  Elements

